Why Early Application Wins
PPF fitted soon after delivery goes onto flawless paint. That means:
No swirls or scratches underneath
Less prep and polishing
Strong, clean bonding
Protection that starts before any chip

Can It Wait a Little?
Yes, a short wait is fine. Many owners book within a month. Do this in the meantime:
Keep the car in a garage or shade
Use hand washing with a soft mitt
Avoid automatic washes
Stay away from parking under trees
Waiting longer lets swirls and chips form. Extra correction then becomes necessary.

Key Benefits of PPF for a New Car
PPF locks in the showroom look. It offers:
Protection from stone chips and light scratches
Minor swirls that heal under heat
Resistance to bird droppings and bugs
Less fading from the sun
Better resale value

Comparing PPF Right After Delivery and PPF Later
Right After Delivery
Paint condition: Most likely excellent, with few marks
Prep needed: Typically a light polish only
Total cost: Often lower
Risk of damage: Small, since protection starts early

Waiting a Few Months
Paint condition: Regular driving can leave swirls, scratches, and chips
Prep needed: Deeper correction before installation
Total cost: Can be higher
Risk of damage: Higher, with the paint open to debris and weather
Key takeaway: PPF early is generally smarter, as it prevents damage. If you delay, a pro inspection and careful prep will still help you get a good finish.

Is PPF an Option for an Old Car?
Yes. The car needs a wash, decontamination, and paint correction first. PPF cannot hide deep scratches or peeling paint, so inspect the car before you book.

